<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-complex-harder-example" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Complex Eigenvalues: A Harder Example</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    On the last page, the eigenvector <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -i \end{bmatrix}</m> had
    fairly simple entries. In general, the entries of the eigenvector can be full complex
    numbers like <m>2 + i</m>, and multiplying them out takes a little bit more work. The strategy from
    <xref ref="thm-complex-strategy"/> is exactly the same, but there is more involved algebra in
    step 2.
  </p>

<p>Before we do the main example on this page, let's make sure you are comfortable with the relevant complex algebra. </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WARM-UP ACTIVITY: FOILING WITH i
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The messy part is multiplying an entry like <m>2 + i</m> into
    <m>\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)</m>. Let's warm up with exactly that computation, in two
    parts.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-foil-complex-entry">
    <title>FOILing a Complex Entry</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We will expand and simplify the product
        <m>(2 + i)\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-foil-expand">
      <title>FOIL Without Simplifying</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          First, FOIL (expand out) the product <m>(2 + i)\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right)</m> into four terms, keeping any <m>i^2</m> as is.
          Which of the following is correct?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>2\cos(3t) + 2i\sin(3t) + i\cos(3t) + i^2\sin(3t)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! The four FOIL terms are: First <m>2 \cdot \cos(3t)</m>, Outer
              <m>2 \cdot i\sin(3t)</m>, Inner <m>i \cdot \cos(3t)</m>, and Last
              <m>i \cdot i\sin(3t) = i^2\sin(3t)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>2\cos(3t) + i^2\sin(3t)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This has the First and Last terms, but FOIL has four terms! The Outer term
              <m>2 \cdot i\sin(3t)</m> and the Inner term <m>i \cdot \cos(3t)</m> are
              missing.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>2\cos(3t) + 2i\sin(3t) + i\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Careful with the Last term: it is <m>i \cdot i\sin(3t)</m>, and the two
              factors of <m>i</m> multiply together to give <m>i^2\sin(3t)</m>, not
              <m>i\sin(3t)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-foil-simplify">
      <title>Simplify with <m>i^2 = -1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now substitute <m>i^2 = -1</m> into
          <m>2\cos(3t) + 2i\sin(3t) + i\cos(3t) + i^2\sin(3t)</m>
          and group the terms with an <m>i</m> separately from the terms without. Which of
          the following is correct?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\left(2\cos(3t) - \sin(3t)\right) + i\left(2\sin(3t) + \cos(3t)\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Since <m>i^2 = -1</m>, the last term becomes <m>-\sin(3t)</m>,
              which has no <m>i</m>, so it joins <m>2\cos(3t)</m> in the real part. The
              terms with a single <m>i</m>, namely <m>2i\sin(3t)</m> and
              <m>i\cos(3t)</m>, group into the imaginary part.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\left(2\cos(3t) + \sin(3t)\right) + i\left(2\sin(3t) + \cos(3t)\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Watch the sign: substituting <m>i^2 = -1</m> into <m>i^2\sin(3t)</m> gives
              <m>-\sin(3t)</m>, so the real part is <m>2\cos(3t) - \sin(3t)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\left(2\cos(3t) - \sin(3t)\right) + i\left(2\sin(3t) - \cos(3t)\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The real part is right, but check the imaginary part: the terms with a
              single <m>i</m> are <m>+2i\sin(3t)</m> and <m>+i\cos(3t)</m>, both with
              plus signs, so the imaginary part is <m>2\sin(3t) + \cos(3t)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MAIN EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now we are ready for the example below!
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-complex-harder">
    <title>A Harder Example with Complex Eigenvalues</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the general solution of the equation
        <m>\dfrac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = A\mathbf{x}</m>, where <m>A</m> has eigenvalues
        <m>\lambda_1 = 2 + 3i</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = 2 - 3i</m> with corresponding
        eigenvectors
        <me>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 + i \\ 3 - 2i \end{bmatrix}
          \qquad \text{and} \qquad
          \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 - i \\ 3 + 2i \end{bmatrix}.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We follow the three steps. Step 1: the complex-valued solution is
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 2 + i \\ 3 - 2i \end{bmatrix}e^{(2 + 3i)t}.</me>
        (Using <m>\lambda_2</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> also works. Just make sure the
        eigenvalue and eigenvector go together!)
      </p>
      <p>
        Step 2: we apply Euler's formula to the exponential,
        <me>e^{(2 + 3i)t} = e^{2t}\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right),</me>
        so
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t)
          = e^{2t}\begin{bmatrix} (2 + i)\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right) \\
            (3 - 2i)\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right) \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Each component needs to be FOILed out and simplified, as in the warm-up. The
        details are in the dropdowns.
      </p>
      <solution> <title>Details: FOILing the first component</title>
      <p>
        This is exactly the warm-up computation:
        <md>
          <mrow>(2 + i)\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right)
            \amp= 2\cos(3t) + 2i\sin(3t) + i\cos(3t) + i^2\sin(3t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \left(2\cos(3t) - \sin(3t)\right) + i\left(2\sin(3t) + \cos(3t)\right).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      </solution>
      <solution> <title>Details: FOILing the second component</title>
      <p>
        Same process, watching the signs from the <m>-2i</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>(3 - 2i)\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right)
            \amp= 3\cos(3t) + 3i\sin(3t) - 2i\cos(3t) - 2i^2\sin(3t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \left(3\cos(3t) + 2\sin(3t)\right) + i\left(3\sin(3t) - 2\cos(3t)\right).</mrow>
        </md>
        Note the term <m>-2i^2\sin(3t) = -2(-1)\sin(3t) = +2\sin(3t)</m>, which is real.
      </p>
      </solution>
      <p>
        Putting the FOILed components back in:
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t)
          = e^{2t}\begin{bmatrix}
            \left(2\cos(3t) - \sin(3t)\right) + i\left(2\sin(3t) + \cos(3t)\right) \\
            \left(3\cos(3t) + 2\sin(3t)\right) + i\left(3\sin(3t) - 2\cos(3t)\right)
          \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Now separating the real and imaginary parts:
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t)
          = \underbrace{e^{2t}\begin{bmatrix} 2\cos(3t) - \sin(3t) \\ 3\cos(3t) + 2\sin(3t) \end{bmatrix}}_{\mathbf{y}}
          + i\underbrace{e^{2t}\begin{bmatrix} 2\sin(3t) + \cos(3t) \\ 3\sin(3t) - 2\cos(3t) \end{bmatrix}}_{\mathbf{z}}.</me>
        Step 3: the general solution is
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}
          = c_1 e^{2t}\begin{bmatrix} 2\cos(3t) - \sin(3t) \\ 3\cos(3t) + 2\sin(3t) \end{bmatrix}
          + c_2 e^{2t}\begin{bmatrix} 2\sin(3t) + \cos(3t) \\ 3\sin(3t) - 2\cos(3t) \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Once again, there is no <m>i</m> anywhere in the final answer.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Same three steps:</alert> messier eigenvectors don't change the strategy,
          only the amount of algebra in step 2.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>FOIL each component:</alert> multiply each eigenvector entry into
          <m>\cos(qt) + i\sin(qt)</m>, substitute <m>i^2 = -1</m>, and group the terms with
          an <m>i</m> separately from the terms without.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Only one pair is needed:</alert> either eigenvalue-eigenvector pair works,
          as long as the eigenvalue and eigenvector go together.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

